WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
[Music]...   [Sneeze]

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.220
[Record skip sound] Hey!

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.080
I'm so sorry...

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.400
Oh, oh my God, oh that's disgusting! Eww.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.060
What if he had a cold? Am I next?

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.400
[Sneeze] During flu and cold season,

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.900
you might notice a lot of sneezing going on.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.660
Viruses, like those that cause colds and influenza,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.980
rely on sneezes as a sneaky way to spread.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.580
They hide in mucus droplets

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.640
that can end up  on the surfaces that you touch.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.460
Or even directly on you,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.680
if you happen to get in the way of the spray.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.920
So, how far away do you have to stand

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.680
to make sure you stay safe?

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.980
Time to Science!

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:43.080
[Snazzy background music]

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.940
Sometimes when you sneeze a fine spray comes out.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.160
Other times big old thick boogers come flopping out.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.740
These thicker boogers have a higher viscosity,

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.780
a measure of how thick and viscous a fluid is,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.800
both thick and thin sneezes can spread germs.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.780
But how does the viscosity of a sneeze

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.720
affect how far it spreads?

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.140
To find out we need to do an experiment.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.940
First we need a hypothesis

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.820
or a prediction that we can test.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.840
This prediction will compare a variable,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.260
something that changes,

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.180
against a control, or something that stays the same.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.940
In our case we want to compare thick vs thin nasal goo.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.240
My hypothesis is that thick viscous slime

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.500
will spatter less and go less far than thin snot will.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.060
But how are we going to test this?

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.200
No two sneezes are alike after all.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.280
In this case, we can actually simplify this down.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.580
We're going to create a model, a representation

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.759
of something that happens in the real world.

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:38.079
We're going to use this little dropper

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.740
as our artificial nose.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.420
We'll fill it with our fake snot

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.960
and squeeze the bulb to make the snot fly.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.060
To measure just how far it goes

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.980
we'll squeeze this bulb and position it over a long tarp --

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.500
which I've marked out in centimeters and meters --

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.000
to show just how far our snot rockets fly.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.220
For our thick fluid I found a recipe

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.080
using corn syrup and gelatin.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.660
It creates this nice, gross, goopy mess.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.020
Snot is actually a mixture

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.000
of water with sugars and proteins.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.920
So corn syrup and gelatin are a pretty good stand-in.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.780
For thin snot I'm just using colored water.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.060
For each type of snot, we load up our droppers,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.500
smack the bulb, and watch the fluid fly.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.040
Then we measure how far it went.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.200
We can also divide up our tarp into sections

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.220
to find out where the fluid concentrates

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.580
for each kind of snot.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.080
This will allow us to create a graph called a histogram.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.920
But hold on a second, we just did that once,

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.200
that's not enough.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.280
If we want to learn about sneezes in general,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.860
we need to run this a lot more times.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.220
How many times?

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.460
I need to do something called a power analysis.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.500
The math behind this can get a little complicated,

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.280
but I use this handy table.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.960
It tells me that in order to compare the differences

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.660
between my two types of snot --

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.440
I need to run each kind 26 times.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.120
Better get to it!

00:02:52.120 --> 00:03:01.680
[music]

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.740
Phew. Now we've got all our data

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.560
and it's time to group it together to find the mean,

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.680
or the average, for the thick snot and the thin snot,

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.320
to find out how far they flew.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.700
Then, we'll run a test called a t-test.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.520
This is a statistical test that we can use to determine

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.400
if the distance that the think snot and the thick snot flew

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.340
was different enough to mean anything.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.240
[music]

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.960
My t-test shows that yes, these are different.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.960
My viscous snot also concentrated much closer

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.420
to the sneezer than my thin snot did.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.720
And my effect sizes show that these differences

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.180
are very large indeed.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.640
When conducting scientific experiments

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.160
it's important to compare results.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.280
Have other scientists studied snot? What did they get?

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.760
Well, a 2016 study showed that larger snot droplets

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.480
spray an average of two meters.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.380
While smaller ones can spray up to 8 meters, or 26 feet!

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.660
A 2005 study also showed that

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.000
increasing the cohesion of mucus droplets,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.280
making them stick together and glop more,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.940
actually decreased the distance

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.240
that the mucus could travel

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.800
and made it less easy to hit a target.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.160
How did ours compare? Well,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.660
our mucus didn't fly quite as well as the 2016 study.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.920
But, that could be because

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.740
our tiny dropper isn't a good substitute for a nose --

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.120
and squeezing the bulb isn't exactly a strong "Achoo!"

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.280
But we did show that changing the viscosity of our fluid

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:21.980
decreased the distance that it could go,

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.680
like the 2005 study.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.940
Keep in mind that most sneezes are a mixture

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.620
of thin snot and thick snot.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.780
So, stand well back regardless.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.620
And the best way to stop a sneeze's spread

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.960
is to cut it off at the source.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.160
So sneezers, cover your nose and mouth,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.240
and wash your hands.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.460
Anything else is snot cool. [laughter]

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.400
Do you have any experiments you want me to try?

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.020
Leave your ideas in the comments,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.640
and don't forget to *subscribe*

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.760
for more awesome experimental videos.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.340
And, if you want to perform this experiment yourself,

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.080
head over to "Eureka! Lab," where you can find

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.220
the full description of how to perform the experiment

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.360
and all the analyses.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.160
The links are right down below.

